<section class="page-header card">
  <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
  <h2>Deadline for Unfinished Essays</h2>
  <p class="lede">DUE is a writing experiment built around a simple rule: every essay ships within 30 days, finished or not. Drafts are public commitments with a clock. Once the deadline expires, the essay goes live and can evolve through later versions.</p>
  <p>For readers, DUE is a place for short, focused essays with visible deadlines, publication status, and version history.</p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h3>Cadence · 30 days to publish</h3>
  <p>Each essay starts with three choices:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>a keyword set;</li>
    <li>a target length band: Tiny, Minute, or Short;</li>
    <li>a fixed publication date, 30 days away.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Before publication, every essay is a <strong>Draft</strong>. When the deadline arrives, an unfinished draft is published as <strong>Unfinished on time</strong>. If the author finishes and publishes before the deadline, it is labeled <strong>Finished on time</strong>.</p>
  <p>After publication, an essay may be revised, but the original deadline and publication outcome remain part of the record.</p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h3>Length · Tiny, Minute, Short</h3>
  <p>Length is part of the constraint. Every essay commits to one of three bands:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tiny</strong><br><strong>250-500 words</strong> · purple <strong>square</strong><br>Short notes, sharp arguments, or single ideas.</li>
    <li><strong>Minute</strong><br><strong>500-1000 words</strong> · orange <strong>triangle</strong><br>Room for a few steps in the argument, plus one or two examples.</li>
    <li><strong>Short</strong><br><strong>1000-1500 words</strong> · teal <strong>circle</strong><br>Short-essay territory, enough space for a structured case.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each essay also shows its exact word count next to the Tiny, Minute, or Short badge.</p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h3>Versions</h3>
  <p>DUE treats essays as evolving objects, not frozen pieces. Version history and release notes document what changed after publication.</p>
  <p>The first public version follows the publication outcome:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Unfinished on time</strong> essays start at <code>0.1.0</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Finished on time</strong> essays start at <code>1.0.0</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Later revisions keep that history visible instead of replacing the original release.</p>
</section>

